{"type": "result", "session_id": "20261004_151755_dd627e", "exit_code": 1, "text": "I was not able to run the command — no output exists to report.\n\nWhat happened, in order:\n\n1. `terminal(\"echo tim68-probe\")` — failed: `can't start new thread`\n2. Same call routed through `execute_code`'s terminal helper — identical error\n3. Bare `subprocess.run([\"echo\", \"tim68-probe\"])` in Python, bypassing the tool wrappers entirely — same `RuntimeError: can't start new thread`\n\nThe third failure is the significant one: it rules out a bug in the terminal tool itself. The host environment is refusing to spawn any new thread or process right now. That typically means the OS-level limit is exhausted — e.g. `RLIMIT_NPROC` (max processes/threads per user) hit, `/proc/sys/kernel/threads-max` or cgroup `pids.max` reached, or memory pressure preventing thread-stack allocation. This is consistent with the earlier note that a background Hermes run on this box appears to be looping.\n\nSo the exact output of `echo tim68-probe` is unavailable, and I won't guess at it. To recover:\n\n- Check whether the stuck background run is still alive and kill it — that should free the process table\n- From a working shell, inspect `ulimit -u`, `cat /proc/sys/kernel/pid_max`, and the cgroup pids limit for this session\n- Once a process slot frees up, `echo tim68-probe` will run normally — the command itself is trivial and the tool path was otherwise fine", "tokens": {"input": 14100, "output": 441, "total": 42317, "cache_read": 27776, "cache_write": 0}, "duration_ms": 57165, "timestamp": 1791127133827}
